{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update27.json\", \"summary\": \"Validated (validate --run <run> --submission update27.json → valid=true). Closed a question raised by the previous VerifyLeader record — that the heartbeat success branch never compares the response term — by reading the other response paths, and recorded it as a protective-mechanism negative. The heartbeat path only refreshes the follower's contact time and hands the response's success bit to pending verifications (replication.go:407-432); but term discovery does not depend on it: the replication loop's commit-timeout trigger sends an AppendEntries to each peer on every interval regardless of whether entries are pending (replication.go:163-172), and that response path compares the peer's term, stopping replication for the peer and signalling a step down when it is higher (replication.go:238-244, 646-650); the pipelined decode path does the same. The heartbeat's own contribution is narrower — keeping the lease's contact record fresh and letting a pending verification conclude (a higher term makes the success bit false, which is submitted as a negative vote, which is exactly how VerifyLeader detects another leader). Recorded as one Fact plus an 'explained' interpretation with the bound stated: stale-term detection is probe-driven, so its latency is bounded by the commit-timeout interval rather than the heartbeat cadence, with the tuning caveat that validation only floors the commit timeout at a millisecond (noted as a consideration, not a separate finding, since the probe still fires and the step-down still follows). Counterevidence kept explicit — a stopped replication state owes no probe and belongs to a peer that is no longer tracked, the heartbeat path does participate when a verification is pending, and a large commit timeout lengthens only the worst case — and the record explicitly notes it does not change the VerifyLeader finding, merely bounds one of its open questions.\"}"}}
